{"schema_version":3,"catalogue_version":1,"id":"bco:35-11","provision":"BCO 35-11","book":"bco","book_name":"Book of Church Order","abbreviation":"BCO","reference":"35-11","title":"Section 35-11","supplementary":false,"canonical_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/provisions/bco/35-11/","reader_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-constitution-reader/#bco/35-11","current_text":{"html":"<p>In like manner, testimony taken by one court and regularly certified shall be received by every other court as no less valid than if it had been taken by itself.</p>","text":"In like manner, testimony taken by one court and regularly certified shall be received by every other court as no less valid than if it had been taken by itself."},"source":{"name":"PCA Constitution Reader","revision":"786a516e34736ad1e66a2d8359907eef9dbb0511","edition":"Current Reader text","files":{"bco.js":"c88e552545b73fd9690de620b237895b7ea52a94bed8e95690c116bf76be8ae5","wcf.js":"1ebf9e54223f7fe4e728a3e640c833a435af825d74b5ef3d4b04687685538a0d","wlc.js":"f6977e761ea4ccc93b07fcacfd0391890cb05b42f4317d807fa293c078d9b051","wsc.js":"dd775417f7ef21e3283bacad5e6ef193c41d64d03bd5a2e8fb934ccca8cb8e53","rao.js":"49808bbb0e95abdc3b07506c87dde0d62d087851e869b49c0f1f2424986450ec"}},"input_fingerprint":"b03938b466f6e2399ef9f5d8b29f442d2b595b98602df319fc7d88f438067d4b","assessment_fingerprint":"517b100d2e9006e313ab6d84d4f0f31593b77832f395383ba2227779470cd034","assessment_summary":{"status":"stale","file":"index/provision_link_adjudications.jsonl","total":11481,"expected":12439,"applied":0,"stale":11481,"unmatched":0,"unassessed":12439},"parent_id":"bco:35","children":[],"coverage":{"relationships":"indexed_records","recommendations":{"status":"incomplete","note":"Recommendation and study papers are not comprehensively indexed by provision."},"history":"partial"},"relationship_count":6,"relationships":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Harrell et al. v. Covenant Presbytery","year":2023,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md:occ-99dd702a505e","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga50_2023__2022-07.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["indictment specificity","sufficiency of indictment","BCO 32-5 times places circumstances","mission church polity","congregational right to select minister","organizing pastor election","fifth commandment","ninth commandment","disqualification of judges","standard of review in appeals","provisional Session authority","particularization of mission church","BCO 31-2 investigation","due process","mission church","particularization","congregational rights","minister selection","witnesses as judges","contumacy","location of trial","reference to presbytery","membership vows","slander","organizing pastor"],"standard_of_review":"factual_findings","review_standards":["factual_findings"],"case_numbers":["2022-7"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"The SJC sustained an appeal by seven members of a 38-member mission church who were convicted by their temporary Session of opposing the organizing minister's expected election as pastor. Reversing the Presbytery's affirmance and the guilty verdicts underlying indefinite suspensions, the SJC held the indictments fatally lacked BCO 32-5 specificity; Presbytery used the wrong standard of review; the Session had no authority to control pastoral selection; and no Fifth or Ninth Commandment offense was proved. Challenges to witness-judges, venue, and refusal to refer were rejected."}},{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Latimer v. Chicago Metro Presbytery","year":2015,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md:occ-e2f3a1536661","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.html","repository_path":"cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":213},"excerpt":"…not indicate what harm this caused or what section of the BCO was violated. BCO 35-11 would seem to imply a prosecutor would certainly not “sit as a judge,\" but it is unclear what the Prosecutor did or did not do while the Presbytery deliberated.","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga43_2015__2013-10.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["unbiblical divorce","deposition from office","ordination vows","divorce filing intent","definite suspension","indefinite suspension","adultery","child custody","sinful intent"],"standard_of_review":"discretion_and_judgment","review_standards":["discretion_and_judgment"],"case_numbers":["2013-10"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A presbytery deposed a minister for violating his seventh vow by pursuing an unbiblical divorce after he filed a civil petition. The SJC sustained the appeal in part: the presbytery clearly erred in finding sin in the filing, because the record did not conclusively show intent to divorce rather than protect his children, and the wife's counter-filing undermined the failure-to-withdraw inference. The SJC rejected prejudice, irregularity, and venue claims, voided the trial judgment, and noted that his expired prior suspension left him a presbytery member without call. Divorce precedents are fact-specific."}},{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Adams v. Northeast Presbytery","year":2001,"disposition":"out_of_order","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md:occ-69378342b8a7","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.html","repository_path":"cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__2000-02.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["complaint during appeal","appellate review","acquittal","retrial","administratively out of order","judicial procedure"],"standard_of_review":"not_reached","case_numbers":["2000-2"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A prosecutor's complaint relating to Northeast Presbytery's judicial case was held administratively out of order by the SJC. The SJC adopted two grounds: BCO 43-1 bars a complaint about a matter subject to an appeal in another case, and the complaint sought retrial of an accused on charges of which he had been acquitted, relief the SJC could not grant. Although the related appeal was later withdrawn and the withdrawal approved, the SJC still applied BCO 43-1. A concurrence disputed that first ground and urged BCO clarification of appellate review of acquittals. No merits ruling was made."}},{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Thomas W. Berry vs. Susquehanna Valley Presbytery","year":null,"disposition":"","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md:occ-c130b2f56aed","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.html","repository_path":"cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":181},"excerpt":"BCO 35-11 states,","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-08.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"case_numbers":["1994-8"],"body":"SJC/CJB"}},{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Wayne E. Wheatley vs. Susquehanna Valley Presbytery","year":null,"disposition":"","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:35-11--case:cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md:occ-c7000030e337","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga23_1995__1994-07.md","locator":{"f